Exactly inverted, and the study's own cited probe is misread. The raw probe log /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-damanpreetsingh-jivo-cli/b2acf681-5cd3-4fc0-92a9-70bab5f9fbf8/scratchpad/probe/detail.jsonl lines 89-90 carry one record per request with its own path and its own response body:
  * GET /order-processing/orders/1/ -> {"id":2,"oms_order_id":1,"order_number":"ORD-20260417-0001"}
  * GET /order-processing/orders/2/ -> {"id":1,"oms_order_id":2,"order_number":"ORD-20260417-0002"}
In both probes the path segment equals oms_order_id and does NOT equal the row `id`. The study was misled by the evidence file's '- probed with real id: 2' line, which is an exporter artifact: export_evidence.py overwrites id_used on every detail record but keeps the FIRST sample ('and not ev[p]["sample"]'), so the md pairs the id=1 body with the id=2 label. The SPA agrees with the API, not with the study: chunks/OrdersPage-B-bx7cVB.js links to:`/order-processing/orders/${s.oms_order_id}` and chunks/DataQualityPage-DgqyZUFq.js does the same. Confidence high — 2/2 probes, and the pair is deliberately swapped (id 1<->oms 2) so it is the ideal discriminator. Shipping this as written makes every `order-processing order <n>` call return the wrong order, silently, with a plausible-looking body.
